### =====================================================================
### §7.7 Report & Moderation
### =====================================================================
###
### Runs against the mock data seeded by init.sql (docker compose up).
### Users:  1 = admin (isAdmin), 2-12 = regular users
### Seeded reports (only #1 and #2 are used below; #3-#5 are already decided
### or unrelated, so they are left alone):
###   #1  Transaction / target_id 1 / reporter 3 / Under_Investigation / handled_by 1
###       └─ Transaction 1: buyer 3, seller 2  → reported party = seller 2
###       └─ already has Appeal #1 (by user 2, Pending)
###   #2  Post / target_id 5 / reporter 6 / Pending
###       └─ Post 5 → Item 5 → owner 5        → reported party = user 5
###   #3  User / target_id 11 / Violation_Confirmed  → already has Appeal #2 (Rejected)
###   #4  Post / target_id 1  / Report_Dismissed
###   #5  User / target_id 9  / Pending
###
### The reported party is resolved by joining the real tables (see
### AppealService.resolveReportedUserId), so these ids come from the DB.
###
### Request bodies use snake_case (those are the DTO field names). Responses
### come straight from Prisma, whose model fields are camelCase — hence
### `$.reportId` / `$.appealId` in the chained references below.

### 1. POST /reports — no auth header -> expect 401
POST {{host}}/reports
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "report_type": "User",
  "target_id": 4,
  "reason_category": "Spam"
}

### 2. POST /reports — with x-user-id -> expect 201 + status: Pending
# @name createReport
POST {{host}}/reports
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 2

{
  "report_type": "User",
  "target_id": 4,
  "reason_category": "Spam",
  "details": "Sending unsolicited messages in chat",
  "evidence_urls": ["https://example.com/evidence1.jpg"]
}

### 3. POST /reports — invalid report_type -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/reports
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 2

{
  "report_type": "NotARealType",
  "target_id": 4,
  "reason_category": "Spam"
}

### 4. GET /admin/reports — no admin header -> expect 403
GET {{host}}/admin/reports
x-user-id: 2

### 5. GET /admin/reports — as admin -> expect 200 + list
GET {{host}}/admin/reports
x-user-id: 1
x-is-admin: true

### 6. POST /admin/reports/:id/resolve — resolve the report from #2 -> expect 200
### run #2 first so the id resolves
POST {{host}}/admin/reports/{{createReport.response.body.$.reportId}}/resolve
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 1
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Violation_Confirmed",
  "resolution_note": "Confirmed after reviewing chat history."
}

### 7. POST /admin/reports/:id/resolve — resolve the SAME report again -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/admin/reports/{{createReport.response.body.$.reportId}}/resolve
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 1
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Report_Dismissed"
}

### 8. POST /admin/reports/999/resolve — nonexistent report -> expect 404
POST {{host}}/admin/reports/999/resolve
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 1
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Violation_Confirmed"
}

### === Appeals — reported party resolved by real DB join ===

### 9. POST /appeals — wrong user appeals the report from #2 -> expect 403
### #2 is report_type User with target_id 4, so only user 4 may appeal it.
POST {{host}}/appeals
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 7

{
  "report_id": {{createReport.response.body.$.reportId}},
  "reason": "Not my report"
}

### 10. POST /appeals — the reported user (4) appeals -> expect 201, status Pending
# @name createAppeal
POST {{host}}/appeals
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 4

{
  "report_id": {{createReport.response.body.$.reportId}},
  "reason": "Those messages were about a legitimate transaction.",
  "evidence_urls": ["https://example.com/appeal-evidence.jpg"]
}

### 11. POST /appeals — appeal the same report twice -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/appeals
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 4

{
  "report_id": {{createReport.response.body.$.reportId}},
  "reason": "Trying again"
}

### 12. POST /admin/appeals/:id/decide — same admin who resolved the report (1) -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/admin/appeals/{{createAppeal.response.body.$.appealId}}/decide
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 1
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Approved"
}

### 13. POST /admin/appeals/:id/decide — a different admin -> expect 200
### Approving also flips the report to Report_Dismissed (verify with #5).
POST {{host}}/admin/appeals/{{createAppeal.response.body.$.appealId}}/decide
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 9
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Approved",
  "resolution_note": "Reviewed chat log, messages were transaction-related."
}

### 14. POST /admin/appeals/:id/decide — decide the SAME appeal again -> expect 400
### Without this guard the appeal would flip to Rejected while its report stayed
### Report_Dismissed, and the original handledBy/handledAt would be overwritten.
POST {{host}}/admin/appeals/{{createAppeal.response.body.$.appealId}}/decide
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 10
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Rejected",
  "resolution_note": "Attempt to overwrite an already-approved appeal"
}

### === Reported-party resolution for Post / Transaction reports ===
### These use the seeded reports, so run them on a fresh `docker compose up`
### (report #2 must still be unresolved for the appeal window check to pass).

### 15. POST /admin/reports/2/resolve — resolve the seeded Post report -> expect 200
POST {{host}}/admin/reports/2/resolve
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 1
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Violation_Confirmed"
}

### 16. POST /appeals — wrong user appeals report 2 -> expect 403
### Report 2 targets Post 5, whose Item is owned by user 5 — user 4 is unrelated.
POST {{host}}/appeals
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 4

{
  "report_id": 2,
  "reason": "Not my post"
}

### 17. POST /appeals — the real Post owner (user 5) appeals -> expect 201
### Resolved via Post 5 -> Item 5 -> ownerId 5.
POST {{host}}/appeals
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 5

{
  "report_id": 2,
  "reason": "The description matched the actual condition."
}

### 18. POST /appeals — reporter of the Transaction report tries to appeal -> expect 403
### Report 1 targets Transaction 1 (buyer 3, seller 2) and was filed by user 3,
### so the reported party is the counterparty (seller 2), not the reporter.
POST {{host}}/appeals
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 3

{
  "report_id": 1,
  "reason": "I filed this report myself"
}


### =====================================================================
### §7.4 Transactions, Fulfillment & Payment
### =====================================================================
###
### AUTH — every endpoint below is behind the real JwtAuthGuard, so each request
### needs:  Authorization: Bearer <accessToken>
###
### ⚠️ The seeded users cannot log in yet. init.sql says the password is
### "password123" but the hash it stores does not actually match it (it is a
### placeholder hash copied from elsewhere). Until that is fixed, mint a token
### locally — run from backend/ with .env loaded:
###
###   node -e "console.log(require('jsonwebtoken').sign({userId:11,email:'x@ku.th',isAdmin:false},process.env.ACCESS_TOKEN_SECRET,{expiresIn:'30m'}))"
###
### Swap userId for whichever party the request needs, then paste below.
### (The correct hash for "password123" is
###  $2b$10$JxMkK98B.uO8UPGOQbQW9ezvclE0FNyDyw1oNNk/vk0ewuNHq8c/a — reported to the team.)
###
### Seeded transactions (docker compose up):
###   #1  post 1  buyer 3   seller 2   Processing      Meetup    → cancel-request flow
###   #2  post 8  buyer 4   seller 8   Completed       Shipping
###   #3  post 5  buyer 11  seller 5   Pending         Meetup    → payment + QR flow
###   #4  post 9  buyer 12  seller 11  Pending Refund  Shipping
###   #5  post 11 buyer 14  seller 12  Processing      Shipping  → ship + delivery flow
###
### Run the groups in order — each one walks a transaction through its states.
### `docker compose down -v && docker compose up -d` resets everything.

@token = PASTE_TOKEN_HERE

### T1. GET /transactions — no token -> expect 401
GET {{host}}/transactions

### T2. GET /transactions — garbage token -> expect 401
GET {{host}}/transactions
Authorization: Bearer garbage

### T3. GET /transactions — only the caller's own transactions come back
GET {{host}}/transactions
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T4. GET /transactions/1 — as a party (buyer 3 or seller 2) -> expect 200
GET {{host}}/transactions/1
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T5. GET /transactions/1 — as an unrelated user (e.g. 9) -> expect 403
GET {{host}}/transactions/1
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T6. GET /transactions/999 — nonexistent -> expect 404
GET {{host}}/transactions/999
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### --- Payment §8.6 — tx 3 starts Pending (buyer 11, seller 5) ---

### T7. POST /payment-slip — as seller 5 -> expect 403 (buyer-only)
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/payment-slip
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "slip_image_url": "https://r2.example.com/slips/3_slip.jpg"
}

### T8. POST /payment-slip — empty body -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/payment-slip
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{}

### T9. POST /payment-slip — as buyer 11 -> expect 200, Pending → Processing
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/payment-slip
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "slip_image_url": "https://r2.example.com/slips/3_slip.jpg"
}

### T10. POST /payment-slip — again, no longer Pending -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/payment-slip
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "slip_image_url": "https://r2.example.com/slips/3_slip.jpg"
}

### T11. POST /confirm-payment-received — as buyer 11 -> expect 403 (seller-only)
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/confirm-payment-received
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T12. POST /confirm-payment-received — as seller 5 -> expect 200
### Stays Processing on purpose: this is only the 1st of the 2 conditions for Completed.
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/confirm-payment-received
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T13. POST /confirm-payment-received — again -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/confirm-payment-received
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### --- Meetup QR §8.6 — continues on tx 3 ---
### The SELLER shows the QR, the BUYER scans it. That direction is what proves
### the goods physically changed hands.

### T14. POST /qr/generate — as buyer 11 -> expect 403 (seller-only)
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/qr/generate
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T15. POST /qr/generate — as seller 5 -> expect 200
### Returns { token, url, expiresInSeconds: 900 }. Stored in Redis at qr_token:3.
# @name generateQr
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/qr/generate
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T16. POST /qr/scan — as seller 5 -> expect 403 (only the buyer may scan)
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/qr/scan
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "token": "{{generateQr.response.body.$.token}}"
}

### T17. POST /qr/scan — wrong token -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/qr/scan
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "token": "wrong-token"
}

### T18. POST /qr/scan — as buyer 11 with the right token -> expect 200
### 2nd condition met → status Completed, Item → Transferred (§8.2),
### and the token is deleted from Redis.
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/qr/scan
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "token": "{{generateQr.response.body.$.token}}"
}

### T19. POST /qr/scan — replay the same token -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/qr/scan
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "token": "{{generateQr.response.body.$.token}}"
}

### --- Shipping §8.6 — tx 5 is Processing (buyer 14, seller 12) ---

### T20. POST /confirm-payment-received — as seller 12 -> expect 200
POST {{host}}/transactions/5/confirm-payment-received
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T21. POST /ship — as buyer 14 -> expect 403 (seller-only)
POST {{host}}/transactions/5/ship
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "tracking_number": "TH1234567890",
  "estimated_delivery_date": "2026-08-20"
}

### T22. POST /ship — malformed date -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/5/ship
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "tracking_number": "TH1234567890",
  "estimated_delivery_date": "not-a-date"
}

### T23. POST /confirm-delivery — before the seller shipped -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/5/confirm-delivery
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T24. POST /ship — as seller 12 -> expect 200, still Processing
### Handing a parcel to a courier is not the buyer receiving it.
POST {{host}}/transactions/5/ship
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "tracking_number": "TH1234567890",
  "estimated_delivery_date": "2026-08-20"
}

### T25. POST /ship — on a Meetup transaction (tx 1) -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/1/ship
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "tracking_number": "TH1234567890",
  "estimated_delivery_date": "2026-08-20"
}

### T26. POST /confirm-delivery — as buyer 14 -> expect 200 → Completed
POST {{host}}/transactions/5/confirm-delivery
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T27. POST /confirm-delivery — again -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/5/confirm-delivery
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### --- Cancellation §8.3 / §8.7 ---
### From Pending either side may cancel outright, because no money has moved.
### From Processing the buyer has already paid, so it needs the other side's
### consent — and the request alone locks every confirm button.
###
### tx 3 and 5 are Completed by now, so seed a fresh Pending row for the
### unilateral case:
###   INSERT INTO "Transaction" (post_id, buyer_id, seller_id, status, delivery_method)
###   VALUES (2, 7, 2, 'Pending', 'Meetup') RETURNING transaction_id;
### It comes back as id 6 on a fresh database. Adjust below if yours differs.

### T28. POST /cancel-request — as an unrelated user -> expect 403
POST {{host}}/transactions/6/cancel-request
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "reason": "Not my transaction"
}

### T29. POST /cancel-request — no reason -> expect 400
### The reason is required: it is the main evidence if this ends in a dispute.
POST {{host}}/transactions/6/cancel-request
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{}

### T30. POST /cancel-request — as buyer 7 while Pending -> expect 200
### Cancels immediately (cancellationResponse comes back "Approved" — no consent
### needed at this stage) and the Item is released back to Listed.
POST {{host}}/transactions/6/cancel-request
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "reason": "เปลี่ยนใจ ยังไม่ได้โอนเงิน"
}

### T31. POST /cancel-request — again -> expect 400 (one request per transaction)
POST {{host}}/transactions/6/cancel-request
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "reason": "Trying again"
}

### --- Cancellation needing consent — tx 1 is Processing (buyer 3, seller 2) ---

### T32. POST /cancel-request/respond — with no request open -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/1/cancel-request/respond
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "response": "Approved"
}

### T33. POST /cancel-request — as buyer 3 while Processing -> expect 200
### NOT cancelled: status stays Processing and cancellationResponse is "Pending".
POST {{host}}/transactions/1/cancel-request
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "reason": "ผู้ขายไม่มาตามนัด"
}

### T34. POST /confirm-payment-received — as seller 2 -> expect 400
### §8.7: an open cancellation request locks the confirm buttons.
POST {{host}}/transactions/1/confirm-payment-received
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T35. POST /qr/generate — as seller 2 -> expect 400 (same lock)
POST {{host}}/transactions/1/qr/generate
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T36. POST /cancel-request/respond — as buyer 3, who made the request -> expect 403
POST {{host}}/transactions/1/cancel-request/respond
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "response": "Approved"
}

### T37. POST /cancel-request/respond — invalid value -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/1/cancel-request/respond
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "response": "Maybe"
}

### T38. POST /cancel-request/respond — "Pending" is the waiting state, not a decision -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/1/cancel-request/respond
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "response": "Pending"
}

### T39. POST /cancel-request/respond — as seller 2, Rejected -> expect 200
### §8.7: rejection does NOT cancel and does NOT auto-resolve. A Transaction
### report is opened (status Under_Investigation) and linked via activeReportId
### so an admin rules on it — and the confirm buttons stay locked meanwhile.
POST {{host}}/transactions/1/cancel-request/respond
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "response": "Rejected"
}

### T40. POST /confirm-payment-received — as seller 2 -> expect 400
### Still locked after the rejection, pending the admin decision.
POST {{host}}/transactions/1/confirm-payment-received
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T41. POST /cancel-request/respond — answer again -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/1/cancel-request/respond
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "response": "Approved"
}

### T42. GET /admin/reports — confirm the auto-created dispute report
### Expect one with reason_category "Cancellation rejected", report_type
### Transaction, target_id 1, status Under_Investigation, and details carrying
### the cancellation reason from T33.
GET {{host}}/admin/reports
x-user-id: 1
x-is-admin: true

### --- Approving a cancellation (the other branch of T39) ---
### Needs a second Processing transaction. Seed one:
###   INSERT INTO "Transaction" (post_id, buyer_id, seller_id, status, delivery_method)
###   VALUES (10, 7, 4, 'Processing', 'Meetup') RETURNING transaction_id;
### Then request as one party and approve as the other: status → Cancelled and
### the Item goes back to Listed.

### T43. POST /cancel-request — as seller 4 -> expect 200, response "Pending"
POST {{host}}/transactions/7/cancel-request
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "reason": "ของหมดสต็อก ขออภัย"
}

### T44. POST /cancel-request/respond — as buyer 7, Approved -> expect 200
### → Cancelled, and the Item is released back to Listed.
POST {{host}}/transactions/7/cancel-request/respond
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "response": "Approved"
}

### --- Refund evidence (§10 issue #2) ---
### These record that the two parties settled up off-platform. They move no
### money, let nobody force a refund, and never change `status`.
### Only valid on a Cancelled transaction where the buyer had actually paid:
###   UPDATE "Transaction" SET payment_confirmed_by_buyer_at = NOW() WHERE transaction_id = 6;

### T45. POST /refund-slip — on a Processing transaction (tx 1) -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/1/refund-slip
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "refund_slip_url": "https://r2.example.com/slips/1_refund.jpg"
}

### T46. POST /refund-slip — as buyer 7 -> expect 403 (the seller refunds)
POST {{host}}/transactions/6/refund-slip
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "refund_slip_url": "https://r2.example.com/slips/6_refund.jpg"
}

### T47. POST /confirm-refund — before any slip was uploaded -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/6/confirm-refund
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T48. POST /refund-slip — as seller 2 -> expect 200, status stays Cancelled
POST {{host}}/transactions/6/refund-slip
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "refund_slip_url": "https://r2.example.com/slips/6_refund.jpg"
}

### T49. POST /confirm-refund — as seller 2 -> expect 403 (the buyer confirms)
POST {{host}}/transactions/6/confirm-refund
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T50. POST /confirm-refund — as buyer 7 -> expect 200, status stays Cancelled
POST {{host}}/transactions/6/confirm-refund
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T51. POST /confirm-refund — again -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/6/confirm-refund
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
